  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...The next round of rain showers is expected for \nthe end of next week, though drought relief is unlikely. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nHigh pressure to the south will build through mid-week, bringing\ndry conditions until late week. Low pressure to the northwest in\nCanada tracks eastward, bringing a relatively weak cold front\nover the state on Friday. Pressure gradient between high pressure\nthe approaching low, help produce some breezy southwest \nconditions Thursday afternoon into Friday. \n\nNot anticipating heavy rain or high rain totals at this time.\nBased on global models, PWATs are roughly 1.25-1.75 inches, \nwith higher values concentrated around the central and Downeast \nregions. Models currently hint at precipitation generally moving\nin late Thursday night from the northwest, and moving through \nthe southeast by Friday mid-morning to afternoon. There is still\nsome model discrepancies in the exact timing, however. Some \nmodels do show moist soundings, but currently not anticipating \ntotals over an inch. Continue to monitor the forecast for \nupdates, as models begin to get a better handle on the \natmospheric set up.",
